1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,520
Imagine it's Friday morning.

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:04,740
You're at the airport, coffee in hand,

3
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:05,800
ready to fly.

4
00:00:06,039 --> 00:00:07,559
And then every screen in the building

5
00:00:07,579 --> 00:00:08,859
turns blue.

6
00:00:08,964 --> 00:00:10,164
The check-in desks.

7
00:00:10,384 --> 00:00:11,484
The departure boards.

8
00:00:11,504 --> 00:00:12,144
The gates.

9
00:00:12,164 --> 00:00:12,944
All of them.

10
00:00:12,964 --> 00:00:14,284
Same blue screen.

11
00:00:14,449 --> 00:00:16,129
And it's not just your airport.

12
00:00:16,409 --> 00:00:18,629
It's happening in banks, hospitals,

13
00:00:18,689 --> 00:00:20,689
supermarkets, TV stations…

14
00:00:20,709 --> 00:00:22,029
all over the world.

15
00:00:22,169 --> 00:00:23,489
At the same time.

16
00:00:23,727 --> 00:00:26,647
This was July 19th, 2024.

17
00:00:26,967 --> 00:00:28,307
And it wasn't a hack.

18
00:00:28,487 --> 00:00:29,807
It was one file.

19
00:00:30,007 --> 00:00:32,707
A file smaller than a photo on your phone.

20
00:00:32,867 --> 00:00:35,727
With one tiny thing too many inside it.

21
00:00:35,804 --> 00:00:38,344
This is how one extra number crashed eight

22
00:00:38,364 --> 00:00:40,084
and a half million computers.

23
00:00:42,428 --> 00:00:44,248
To understand what happened,

24
00:00:44,328 --> 00:00:45,748
you need to meet the guard.

25
00:00:45,930 --> 00:00:46,530
Hello.

26
00:00:46,550 --> 00:00:47,950
I protect your computer.

27
00:00:48,050 --> 00:00:49,590
Very important job.

28
00:00:49,795 --> 00:00:53,115
Big companies don't just use normal antivirus.

29
00:00:53,495 --> 00:00:55,235
They use security software like

30
00:00:55,255 --> 00:00:56,935
CrowdStrike Falcon,

31
00:00:57,155 --> 00:00:59,435
a guard that watches everything happening

32
00:00:59,455 --> 00:01:02,055
on the computer, all the time.

33
00:01:02,081 --> 00:01:04,121
And to see everything, the guard doesn't

34
00:01:04,141 --> 00:01:05,761
stand at the front door.

35
00:01:05,941 --> 00:01:07,501
It lives in the basement.

36
00:01:07,710 --> 00:01:09,830
That basement is called the kernel.

37
00:01:10,210 --> 00:01:11,970
It's the deepest part of Windows,

38
00:01:12,170 --> 00:01:14,210
where the computer talks directly to its

39
00:01:14,230 --> 00:01:15,250
own hardware.

40
00:01:15,507 --> 00:01:18,707
Down there, the guard can stop almost any attack.

41
00:01:18,787 --> 00:01:19,967
But there's a catch.

42
00:01:20,147 --> 00:01:22,207
In the kernel, there's no such thing as a

43
00:01:22,227 --> 00:01:23,547
small mistake.

44
00:01:23,963 --> 00:01:26,643
If a normal app crashes, the app closes.

45
00:01:27,063 --> 00:01:28,923
If something in the kernel crashes,

46
00:01:29,123 --> 00:01:31,934
Windows has no safe way to keep going.

47
00:01:31,954 --> 00:01:34,454
So it does the only safe thing it can.

48
00:01:34,634 --> 00:01:35,954
It stops everything.

49
00:01:36,194 --> 00:01:37,914
And shows you a blue screen.

50
00:01:38,524 --> 00:01:41,564
Now, hackers invent new tricks every day.

51
00:01:41,744 --> 00:01:43,804
So the guard needs new instructions every

52
00:01:43,824 --> 00:01:44,884
day too.

53
00:01:44,942 --> 00:01:47,202
CrowdStrike sends those instructions in

54
00:01:47,222 --> 00:01:49,782
small update files, straight to millions

55
00:01:49,802 --> 00:01:50,802
of computers.

56
00:01:51,402 --> 00:01:52,162
No restart.

57
00:01:52,642 --> 00:01:54,062
No button to click.

58
00:01:54,102 --> 00:01:55,062
They just arrive.

59
00:01:55,364 --> 00:01:56,984
Think of it like a delivery.

60
00:01:57,564 --> 00:01:59,524
Every package comes with a form,

61
00:01:59,544 --> 00:02:01,884
and the guard knows exactly how many boxes

62
00:02:01,904 --> 00:02:03,424
are on that form.

63
00:02:03,555 --> 00:02:05,775
For one type of form, the guard expected

64
00:02:05,795 --> 00:02:07,215
twenty boxes.

65
00:02:07,662 --> 00:02:10,142
At 4:09 in the morning, UTC,

66
00:02:10,422 --> 00:02:12,162
a new update went out.

67
00:02:12,322 --> 00:02:14,222
And that form had twenty-one.

68
00:02:14,292 --> 00:02:15,092
Seventeen.

69
00:02:15,112 --> 00:02:15,792
Eighteen.

70
00:02:15,812 --> 00:02:16,632
Nineteen.

71
00:02:16,652 --> 00:02:17,372
Twenty…

72
00:02:17,622 --> 00:02:18,502
Twenty…

73
00:02:18,622 --> 00:02:19,262
one?

74
00:02:19,784 --> 00:02:21,504
When the guard reached for box number

75
00:02:21,524 --> 00:02:24,044
twenty-one, it reached into memory that

76
00:02:24,064 --> 00:02:25,624
didn't belong to it.

77
00:02:25,684 --> 00:02:27,504
And in the kernel, that's fatal.

78
00:02:27,781 --> 00:02:28,561
Crash.

79
00:02:29,296 --> 00:02:31,016
But here's the cruel part.

80
00:02:31,256 --> 00:02:32,676
When the computer restarted,

81
00:02:32,696 --> 00:02:35,096
the guard woke up first, read the same

82
00:02:35,116 --> 00:02:36,336
file again…

83
00:02:36,462 --> 00:02:37,602
…and crashed again.

84
00:02:37,622 --> 00:02:38,362
And again.

85
00:02:38,382 --> 00:02:39,202
And again.

86
00:02:39,831 --> 00:02:41,951
Within minutes, the blue screens spread

87
00:02:41,971 --> 00:02:43,251
across the planet.

88
00:02:43,373 --> 00:02:44,773
Flights were grounded.

89
00:02:45,013 --> 00:02:47,273
Hospitals went back to pen and paper.

90
00:02:47,633 --> 00:02:48,973
Payment machines stopped.

91
00:02:49,253 --> 00:02:51,733
Some TV channels went off the air.

92
00:02:51,798 --> 00:02:54,558
Microsoft later estimated that eight and a

93
00:02:54,578 --> 00:02:57,378
half million Windows computers went down.

94
00:02:57,501 --> 00:02:59,261
And here's the crazy part:

95
00:02:59,541 --> 00:03:01,321
that was less than one percent of all

96
00:03:01,341 --> 00:03:02,641
Windows machines.

97
00:03:03,095 --> 00:03:04,635
Less than one percent.

98
00:03:04,855 --> 00:03:06,355
But it was the one percent that runs

99
00:03:06,375 --> 00:03:08,615
airports, banks and hospitals.

100
00:03:09,275 --> 00:03:11,115
If this kind of story is your thing,

101
00:03:11,135 --> 00:03:12,115
hit subscribe.

102
00:03:12,635 --> 00:03:15,035
It's free, and unlike this update,

103
00:03:15,055 --> 00:03:16,535
it won't crash anything.

104
00:03:16,842 --> 00:03:19,422
CrowdStrike spotted the problem fast.

105
00:03:19,862 --> 00:03:21,662
About an hour and twenty minutes after the

106
00:03:21,682 --> 00:03:23,813
update went out, they pulled it.

107
00:03:23,833 --> 00:03:25,433
Problem solved, right?

108
00:03:25,634 --> 00:03:26,834
Not even close.

109
00:03:27,274 --> 00:03:28,974
Because a computer stuck in a crash loop

110
00:03:28,994 --> 00:03:30,714
can't download a fix.

111
00:03:31,105 --> 00:03:33,365
So the fix had to be done by hand.

112
00:03:33,625 --> 00:03:35,465
Someone had to walk up to each broken

113
00:03:35,485 --> 00:03:38,385
computer, start it in safe mode,

114
00:03:38,405 --> 00:03:40,584
find one file, and delete it.

115
00:03:40,604 --> 00:03:42,504
One computer at a time.

116
00:03:42,704 --> 00:03:45,184
Now imagine you're the IT person,

117
00:03:45,244 --> 00:03:47,904
and your company has fifty thousand of them.

118
00:03:47,997 --> 00:03:50,117
And many of those machines were encrypted

119
00:03:50,137 --> 00:03:51,557
with BitLocker.

120
00:03:51,837 --> 00:03:54,277
So before you could even delete the file,

121
00:03:54,337 --> 00:03:56,537
you needed a long recovery key.

122
00:03:56,717 --> 00:03:58,297
For every single machine.

123
00:03:58,705 --> 00:04:00,885
Some companies reportedly kept those keys

124
00:04:00,905 --> 00:04:02,505
on servers…

125
00:04:02,525 --> 00:04:03,745
that had also crashed.

126
00:04:04,442 --> 00:04:05,662
It took days.

127
00:04:05,922 --> 00:04:08,682
CrowdStrike said that by July 29th,

128
00:04:08,702 --> 00:04:10,122
ten days later,

129
00:04:10,282 --> 00:04:12,182
about ninety-nine percent of its Windows

130
00:04:12,202 --> 00:04:14,142
sensors were back online.

131
00:04:14,468 --> 00:04:17,168
So how much did one extra number cost?

132
00:04:17,349 --> 00:04:19,569
Delta Air Lines says it had to cancel

133
00:04:19,589 --> 00:04:21,829
about seven thousand flights,

134
00:04:21,869 --> 00:04:24,389
affecting more than a million passengers.

135
00:04:25,049 --> 00:04:27,509
It sued CrowdStrike for over five hundred

136
00:04:27,529 --> 00:04:28,909
million dollars.

137
00:04:29,109 --> 00:04:31,849
CrowdStrike says Delta's own slow recovery

138
00:04:31,869 --> 00:04:34,069
was the real problem.

139
00:04:34,298 --> 00:04:36,498
An insurance firm called Parametrix

140
00:04:36,518 --> 00:04:39,138
estimated that the biggest US companies

141
00:04:39,158 --> 00:04:43,638
alone lost about five point four billion dollars.

142
00:04:43,894 --> 00:04:45,654
And to say sorry to the partners who

143
00:04:45,674 --> 00:04:48,374
helped clean up, CrowdStrike sent them a

144
00:04:48,394 --> 00:04:50,954
ten dollar Uber Eats gift card.

145
00:04:51,194 --> 00:04:53,114
Which some people couldn't use…

146
00:04:53,134 --> 00:04:55,094
because Uber flagged it as fraud.

147
00:04:55,826 --> 00:04:56,966
Ten dollars.

148
00:04:56,986 --> 00:04:59,186
For a five billion dollar Friday.

149
00:04:59,591 --> 00:05:01,811
So how did a mistake this simple get past

150
00:05:01,831 --> 00:05:03,451
a company this big?

151
00:05:03,488 --> 00:05:05,498
According to CrowdStrike's own report,

152
00:05:05,518 --> 00:05:08,038
the tool that was supposed to check these

153
00:05:08,058 --> 00:05:10,238
files had a bug of its own.

154
00:05:10,418 --> 00:05:12,398
It let the bad file through.

155
00:05:12,512 --> 00:05:15,280
And the update went to everyone at once.

156
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:16,673
No small test group first.

157
00:05:16,693 --> 00:05:18,282
No slow rollout.

158
00:05:18,537 --> 00:05:20,917
Since then, CrowdStrike says it now checks

159
00:05:20,937 --> 00:05:22,397
the number of fields,

160
00:05:22,577 --> 00:05:24,377
sends updates in rings,

161
00:05:24,637 --> 00:05:26,977
a small group first, then everyone,

162
00:05:27,157 --> 00:05:29,877
and lets customers choose when they get them.

163
00:05:29,970 --> 00:05:32,290
The lesson is bigger than one company.

164
00:05:32,590 --> 00:05:34,450
The more of the world runs on the same

165
00:05:34,470 --> 00:05:35,550
software,

166
00:05:35,690 --> 00:05:38,490
the smaller the mistake it takes to stop it.

167
00:05:38,804 --> 00:05:40,424
So here's my question.

168
00:05:40,744 --> 00:05:42,144
Who's really to blame?

169
00:05:42,524 --> 00:05:44,444
The company that shipped the file…

170
00:05:44,464 --> 00:05:46,364
or a world that let one update reach

171
00:05:46,384 --> 00:05:48,604
millions of computers at once?

172
00:05:48,924 --> 00:05:50,164
Tell me in the comments.

173
00:05:50,399 --> 00:05:51,339
And subscribe,

174
00:05:51,479 --> 00:05:53,759
because next time, people found a way to

175
00:05:53,779 --> 00:05:56,039
steal Instagram accounts…

176
00:05:56,059 --> 00:05:58,139
by politely asking an AI.

177
00:05:58,565 --> 00:05:59,725
Twenty boxes.

178
00:05:59,745 --> 00:06:00,805
Always twenty.

179
00:06:00,905 --> 00:06:02,065
I check now.
